WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:10.140
So I want you to try and imagine a world where the money in your pocket or, you know, the digital numbers sitting in your checking account aren't backed by what we traditionally think of as collateral.

00:10.620 --> 00:13.260
Right, because we are just so used to the current paradigms.

00:13.260 --> 00:22.320
Exactly. We just sort of accept that our money is backed by government fiat, which is, I mean, essentially just the promise of future tax revenues and institutional trust.

00:22.320 --> 00:30.180
Yeah, or if you look historically, we always think of it being backed by a vault of gold sitting in some, you know, subterranean reserve somewhere.

00:30.420 --> 00:41.280
Totally. Or even if you're looking at the modern decentralized web, you might imagine money backed by artificial computational scarcity, like a cryptocurrency algorithm churning through massive math problems.

00:41.600 --> 00:43.720
Which is a whole other kind of abstract backing.

00:43.860 --> 00:48.600
It is. But I want you to set literally all of those models aside for the next hour or so.

00:48.600 --> 00:59.660
Instead, I want you to imagine that the literal existence of the money you're carrying is backed by the physical, undeniable reality of, say, a newly reinforced concrete foundation on your street.

01:00.080 --> 01:00.920
Okay, wow.

01:01.240 --> 01:03.560
Yeah. Or like a brand new apartment building downtown.

01:04.300 --> 01:08.620
Or even an extended municipal sewer line that was just inspected and approved yesterday.

01:09.000 --> 01:14.280
It is a really profound, almost dizzying shift in how we conceptualize value.

01:14.280 --> 01:20.160
I mean, we have become entirely accustomed to money representing this abstract claim.

01:20.880 --> 01:24.280
Or being tethered to a purely market-driven appraisal of an asset.

01:24.920 --> 01:32.480
We live in this economy of financialization where the map has basically largely replaced the territory.

01:32.620 --> 01:34.260
The map replaces the territory, exactly.

01:34.940 --> 01:41.720
But what if money was fundamentally, inextricably tethered to verifiable physical improvements in the built environment?

01:41.720 --> 01:50.020
Like, what if the financial ledger couldn't move unless physical matter in the real world was rearranged in a useful, certified way?

01:50.320 --> 01:52.460
And that is exactly the premise of today's deep dive.

01:52.680 --> 01:58.780
We are unpacking a genuinely fascinating, incredibly dense, and sometimes, honestly, mind-bending paper.

01:59.060 --> 02:04.740
It was published in October 2026 by an independent researcher going by the name Flyxion.

02:05.100 --> 02:05.800
Yeah, Flyxion.

02:05.800 --> 02:11.560
The paper is titled, Issuance Against Improvement, A Monetary Theory of Certified Built Capacity.

02:12.200 --> 02:18.040
And our mission today is to tear this thing apart, just like the author actually does to their own idea, which is so refreshing to read.

02:18.160 --> 02:18.660
It really is.

02:18.860 --> 02:28.100
We're going to explore how this paper tries to fix the endless, exhausting boom-and-bust cycle of real estate that I think literally everyone listening is painfully aware of.

02:28.180 --> 02:29.520
Oh, intimately aware of.

02:29.520 --> 02:39.160
Yeah, we're going to look at the surprising logical traps the author discovers along the way, and then we'll dive into the brilliant pivot the paper makes to actually solve those exact traps.

02:39.340 --> 02:42.840
And it is a super necessary journey to take with this author.

02:43.560 --> 02:50.520
Because they don't just present this utopian theory and ask us to take it on faith, which happens way too often in economics papers.

02:50.520 --> 02:51.200
Oh, all the time.

02:51.320 --> 02:51.500
Right.

02:52.540 --> 02:55.220
They stress test their own framework rigorously.

02:55.560 --> 03:07.640
They build this theoretical architecture for physical money, break it down by brutally exposing its fatal flaws, and then they rebuild it into a credit architecture that is surprisingly highly practical.

03:08.160 --> 03:08.860
So practical.

03:08.860 --> 03:13.760
The central question organizing this entire exploration is basically this.

03:14.440 --> 03:20.760
What monetary claims should a verified transformation of the built environment actually be permitted to originate?

03:21.140 --> 03:22.160
Which is a huge question.

03:22.820 --> 03:36.160
So whether you are obsessed with macroeconomics, fascinated by real estate, or if you're just a normal person who's completely tired of the housing affordability crisis and the feeling that the whole economy is basically a casino.

03:36.460 --> 03:37.900
A rigged casino at times.

03:37.900 --> 03:38.380
Yeah.

03:39.460 --> 03:44.300
This deep dive is going to completely reframe how you think about the physical world around you.

03:44.960 --> 03:54.840
So before we get into the heavy weeds of how this new capacity-backed system works, we really have to establish the massive glaring problem it is trying to solve in our current financial system.

03:55.120 --> 03:55.540
We do.

03:55.860 --> 03:59.880
And the paper calls it the collateral channel of conventional property finance.

04:00.060 --> 04:00.340
Okay.

04:00.740 --> 04:01.580
A collateral channel.

04:02.220 --> 04:03.060
Unpack that for us.

04:03.060 --> 04:08.280
Well, to understand the cure this paper proposes, you really have to understand the disease deeply.

04:08.800 --> 04:15.640
In our conventional, everyday, mortgage-backed regime, there is a very clear, very dangerous feedback loop.

04:15.860 --> 04:16.140
Right.

04:16.140 --> 04:18.480
Let's walk through how it operates mechanically.

04:19.240 --> 04:21.500
Let's say a neighborhood suddenly becomes fashionable.

04:22.100 --> 04:25.000
This could be for literally any number of reasons.

04:25.140 --> 04:26.660
Like a cultural shift happens.

04:27.060 --> 04:27.440
Exactly.

04:27.440 --> 04:35.520
A cool coffee shop opens, or a massive tech company moves its headquarters nearby, or maybe the city simply re-zones a nearby district.

04:36.480 --> 04:40.060
The subjective market demand for that specific land goes up.

04:40.700 --> 04:41.740
People want to live there.

04:41.740 --> 04:43.860
So, the price of the land rises.

04:44.420 --> 04:48.040
Which, you know, on the surface, just sounds like basic economics, supply and demand.

04:48.460 --> 04:51.460
The place gets popular, so it just costs more to buy a house there.

04:51.920 --> 04:52.560
On the surface?

04:52.840 --> 04:53.620
Yeah, absolutely.

04:54.300 --> 04:59.380
But here is where the financial engineering takes over and completely distorts the reality.

04:59.380 --> 05:13.580
Because that land is now priced higher based on subjective demand, the buyers who want to purchase property there, or even the current owners who want to refinance, they can use that higher valuation to take out larger loans from banks.

05:14.140 --> 05:15.100
Oh, right.

05:15.420 --> 05:17.140
Because the bank sees the new price tag.

05:17.420 --> 05:17.820
Exactly.

05:18.560 --> 05:19.800
The banks look at the appraisal.

05:20.160 --> 05:22.500
They look at the recent comparable sales in the neighborhood.

05:23.120 --> 05:24.000
They see a higher number.

05:24.560 --> 05:28.960
And they say, well, the collateral is worth more, so we can lend you more money against it.

05:28.960 --> 05:30.640
And here is the kicker for that.

05:31.080 --> 05:40.620
That increased purchasing power, because buyers now literally have bigger loans in their pockets, newly created bank credit that causes the land prices to rise even more.

05:40.800 --> 05:46.060
Because now you have buyers bidding against each other with larger and larger pools of debt.

05:46.060 --> 05:48.680
It's an audio feedback loop.

05:49.460 --> 05:50.740
Oh, that's a great way to put it.

05:51.080 --> 05:55.440
I always think of it like placing a live microphone way too close to a speaker on a stage.

05:55.620 --> 05:58.600
You know, the speaker outputs a sound, the microphone picks it up.

05:58.600 --> 06:00.220
Sends it back to the amplifier.

06:00.500 --> 06:02.280
The speaker blasts it out louder.

06:02.820 --> 06:03.860
The mic picks it up again.

06:03.940 --> 06:08.020
And it just endlessly amplifies itself until it screeches and blows the whole amp.

06:08.480 --> 06:10.200
That analogy captures the mechanics perfectly.

06:11.360 --> 06:21.120
And the paper notes that the historical record of mortgage lending suggests this specific channel has become quantitatively central to modern financial systems globally.

06:21.540 --> 06:22.100
Globally.

06:22.100 --> 06:23.340
Yeah, it's everywhere.

06:24.620 --> 06:28.760
Asset prices and borrowing capacity endlessly amplify one another.

06:29.260 --> 06:30.960
It's not just a quirk of the market.

06:31.100 --> 06:33.940
It is the engine of the modern housing crisis.

06:34.060 --> 06:34.200
Yeah.

06:34.200 --> 06:47.340
We see household balance sheet evidence from the U.S. and other developed nations showing that borrowing against rising home equity vastly expands household leverage without any corresponding increase in actual economic productivity.

06:47.720 --> 06:49.120
Which is terrifying.

06:49.580 --> 06:53.380
We're just creating debt out of thin air because we all agree a house is cooler now.

06:53.380 --> 06:54.000
Exactly.

06:54.800 --> 06:57.660
The paper describes this as a collateral price regime.

06:58.280 --> 07:02.760
And the author actually includes a dimensionless simulation to illustrate this mathematically.

07:03.080 --> 07:03.480
Oh, right.

07:03.560 --> 07:04.500
I remember that chart.

07:04.720 --> 07:04.840
Yeah.

07:04.880 --> 07:15.020
In a collateral price regime where credit is a linear function of price appreciation and excess credit raises prices in turn, the deviation obeys a recursion.

07:15.560 --> 07:17.540
The loop produces geometric acceleration.

07:18.060 --> 07:21.260
The curve on the chart just bends upward exponentially until it collapses.

07:21.260 --> 07:24.480
The screech of the feedback loop.

07:24.880 --> 07:29.480
I mean, the 2008 financial crisis was essentially the amp blowing out on a global scale.

07:30.240 --> 07:35.340
So the author of this paper looks at this screeching amp and proposes cutting the loop entirely.

07:35.820 --> 07:36.540
But how?

07:36.700 --> 07:41.260
If you don't base loans on what the market says a house is worth, what on earth do you base them on?

07:41.880 --> 07:46.300
By fundamentally changing what justifies the creation of a financial claim in the first place.

07:46.300 --> 07:54.660
The author introduces a concept initially framed as a new currency unit called the built capacity unit or BCU.

07:55.280 --> 07:56.300
The BCU.

07:56.560 --> 07:56.780
Yeah.

07:57.020 --> 08:01.560
And the core rule, the absolute bedrock of this idea, is this aphorism.

08:02.560 --> 08:03.780
Nothing physically changed.

08:04.340 --> 08:06.200
Therefore, nothing monetary changed.

08:06.200 --> 08:11.580
I want to pause on that because it's such a clean, almost radical departure from how we live today.

08:12.340 --> 08:13.880
Nothing physically changed.

08:14.240 --> 08:16.000
Therefore, nothing monetary changed.

08:16.140 --> 08:17.900
Let's actually apply that to the real world.

08:18.140 --> 08:18.720
Okay, let's do it.

08:19.140 --> 08:29.560
If I own a house and its value doubles on some real estate app over three years just because my city became a trendy place to move, zero new money is issued in this system.

08:29.700 --> 08:29.920
Zero.

08:29.920 --> 08:32.000
Because I didn't do anything.

08:32.280 --> 08:36.120
The house is the exact same arrangement of wood and drywall it was three years ago.

08:36.260 --> 08:39.560
If anything, the roof is actually three years older and more degraded.

08:39.780 --> 08:39.880
Right.

08:40.020 --> 08:41.000
Physically, it's worth less.

08:41.120 --> 08:41.460
Exactly.

08:42.120 --> 08:51.780
But if I build an extension or if I hire contractors to, like, rip out my foundation and seismically retrofit it, a physical transformation has occurred.

08:52.040 --> 08:55.500
I have increased the capacity of the built environment.

08:55.880 --> 08:57.500
And then new money is issued.

08:57.500 --> 09:01.140
And the paper formalizes this physical requirement with an equation.

09:01.820 --> 09:08.660
The author defines the certified capacity at a given location, let's just call the location X, with a highly specific formula.

09:08.960 --> 09:14.680
The formula is C of X equals A of X times Q of X times L of X.

09:15.100 --> 09:15.560
Okay, wait.

09:16.020 --> 09:16.820
Let me stop you right there.

09:16.980 --> 09:21.620
We promised everyone listening no overwhelming academic jargon without translation.

09:21.840 --> 09:24.340
So we need to break down that math into plain English.

09:24.580 --> 09:25.000
Fair enough.

09:25.000 --> 09:27.880
It's actually quite intuitive when you separate the variables out.

09:28.540 --> 09:31.280
C is the total certified capacity of a structure.

09:31.740 --> 09:34.580
This is the ultimate number you are measuring to issue the currency.

09:34.760 --> 09:35.620
Okay, C is capacity.

09:36.100 --> 09:36.820
Got it.

09:37.460 --> 09:41.440
Then A stands for the legally permitted usable floor area.

09:42.000 --> 09:46.440
This is simply the square footage that you can actually inhabit or use for commerce.

09:46.700 --> 09:47.200
Makes sense.

09:47.640 --> 09:49.080
The bigger the building, the more capacity.

09:49.080 --> 09:49.700
Exactly.

09:50.700 --> 09:54.260
Then Q is a code compliance or infrastructure factor.

09:54.880 --> 09:58.440
This measures how well the building meets actual safety and engineering standards.

09:58.980 --> 10:00.300
Is it fire resistant?

10:00.660 --> 10:02.380
Can it withstand a certain wind load?

10:02.780 --> 10:03.960
So quality and safety.

10:04.420 --> 10:04.680
Yes.

10:05.300 --> 10:08.120
And finally, L is a location coefficient.

10:08.740 --> 10:09.680
Wait, hold on.

10:10.180 --> 10:11.260
A location coefficient.

10:11.260 --> 10:14.860
Doesn't that just bring us right back to the trendy neighborhood problem?

10:14.860 --> 10:19.100
If L is high because the neighborhood has a cool vibe, the loop is back.

10:19.480 --> 10:21.140
The microphone is right back next to the speaker.

10:21.760 --> 10:27.400
That is exactly where you have to look closely at how the author defines L in this specific formula.

10:28.200 --> 10:33.560
This location coefficient is intended to be built strictly from physical, measurable quantities.

10:33.560 --> 10:36.580
Oh, physical quantities, not vibes.

10:36.960 --> 10:37.120
Right.

10:37.460 --> 10:41.000
We are not talking about subjective desirability here.

10:41.200 --> 10:55.820
We are talking about physical access to roads, the bandwidth of municipal utility hookups, the measurable distance to mass transit networks, emergency response times based on street geometry, the flow rate of water and sewer capacity.

10:56.100 --> 10:56.620
Oh, wow.

10:56.940 --> 11:00.700
Even quantified climate risk like elevation above a floodplain.

11:00.700 --> 11:06.440
It is emphatically, explicitly not based on speculative land prices.

11:07.060 --> 11:09.580
So it's geography as pure infrastructure.

11:10.220 --> 11:16.020
It's geography as a network of pipes and roads and flood zones, not geography as a cultural destination.

11:16.460 --> 11:16.660
Yes.

11:17.280 --> 11:27.800
The monetary base of the entire economy in this initial thought experiment of the paper is then defined as a fraction of the sum of all this certified capacity across all locations.

11:28.060 --> 11:29.060
That is wild.

11:29.460 --> 11:29.840
It is.

11:29.840 --> 11:36.680
New money is created only when physical capacity is created, not when the exchange price of existing capacity magically rises.

11:36.960 --> 11:42.640
When you put it that way, you really can see the sheer power of narrowing the definition of value.

11:43.260 --> 11:45.020
Think about a standard home appraisal today.

11:45.360 --> 11:46.880
It is inherently subjective.

11:47.400 --> 11:53.920
An appraiser walks through your house and basically looks at comparables like what did the guy three streets over pay for his house last month?

11:54.160 --> 11:54.380
Right.

11:54.460 --> 11:56.560
It's based entirely on market bids.

11:56.800 --> 11:57.280
Exactly.

11:57.280 --> 12:05.920
But building codes, things like fire separations, load paths for gravity and seismic events, egress routes, ventilation rates.

12:05.920 --> 12:08.440
Those are objective, checkable facts.

12:08.600 --> 12:11.080
You literally can't fake a fire-raided door.

12:11.300 --> 12:13.600
It either meets the physical standard or it doesn't.

12:13.600 --> 12:19.420
The paper actually makes a brilliant observation here about epistemology, like how we know what we know.

12:20.080 --> 12:25.400
It points out that building codes contain a primitive ontology of real economic things.

12:25.400 --> 12:26.720
Say more about that.

12:27.240 --> 12:31.440
Well, an inspector working in this system isn't asked, what is this property worth?

12:32.240 --> 12:37.940
Asking that question just reintroduces the appraisal feedback loop, relying on the opinions of market actors.

12:38.820 --> 12:42.240
Instead, the inspector is asked a purely factual question.

12:43.200 --> 12:51.060
Did a specified physical transformation occur and does the resulting structure satisfy publicly stated engineering and safety conditions?

12:51.060 --> 12:53.680
I love that distinction so much.

12:53.680 --> 13:00.160
You could have two houses with identical structural capacity, same square footage, same material, same fire rating.

13:00.880 --> 13:05.740
Under our current system, one might be priced at triple the other just because of where it stands.

13:05.860 --> 13:08.320
Maybe it has a nicer view of some mountains.

13:08.320 --> 13:09.000
Exactly.

13:09.400 --> 13:14.740
But a system that tracks physical improvement doesn't treat that scarcity premium as a physical fact.

13:15.160 --> 13:18.380
It treats both houses as having identical built capacity.

13:19.240 --> 13:27.660
And to manage all this data without relying on market prices, the author leans on a very old institutional concept, but fundamentally augments it.

13:28.300 --> 13:31.260
They introduce the idea of expanding the cadaster.

13:31.640 --> 13:35.600
For anyone unfamiliar, a cadaster is basically just a land registry, right?

13:35.800 --> 13:36.120
Correct.

13:36.120 --> 13:43.060
Land registries and cadastral surveys have long been the primary way states make parcels of land legally legible.

13:43.780 --> 13:51.440
For centuries, they've attached rights, ownership, and tax obligations to specific geometric coordinates on a physical map.

13:51.620 --> 13:51.840
Right.

13:52.020 --> 13:59.800
But the author suggests coupling this traditional map-based cadaster with an append-only record of permitted inspected transformations.

14:00.200 --> 14:02.740
It creates a chain of physical provenance.

14:03.160 --> 14:04.220
How does the chain work?

14:04.860 --> 14:05.620
It goes like this.

14:05.620 --> 14:21.120
You start with a legal parcel, which links to a building permit, which links to the procurement of materials and physical labor, which links to a municipal inspection, which links to a certificate of occupancy, which finally results in a capacity credit.

14:21.120 --> 14:25.260
It grounds the entire financial ledger in the physical territory.

14:25.420 --> 14:32.580
The ledger just can't float away into the speculative stratosphere because it is heavily anchored by tons of concrete and steel.

14:33.120 --> 14:37.760
Honestly, listening to this first section of the paper, it sounds incredible.

14:38.280 --> 14:38.740
It really does.

14:38.900 --> 14:46.360
It sounds like a grounded physical utopia where we finally solve the housing crisis by making money represent real work again.

14:46.360 --> 14:47.780
It does sound utopian.

14:48.500 --> 14:51.240
The logic appears completely airtight at first glance.

14:52.100 --> 14:52.220
But.

14:52.780 --> 15:00.000
And here is where the paper takes a wild, wild turn, because the author doesn't just stop there and publish this as a finished proposal.

15:00.740 --> 15:07.500
Having built this beautiful, intricate, theoretical sandcastle, the author steps back and systematically kicks it to pieces.

15:07.920 --> 15:08.540
They really do.

15:08.540 --> 15:10.020
It's amazing to read.

15:10.640 --> 15:18.780
We transition into the second stage of the paper's analysis, where we discover what the author calls the three fatal flaws of literal capacity backed money.

15:19.300 --> 15:24.780
We really have to look closely at why physical buildings actually make terrible literal currency.

15:25.640 --> 15:28.260
OK, so this physical utopia sounds bulletproof to me.

15:28.360 --> 15:29.020
Where does it crack?

15:29.620 --> 15:31.380
Let's get into the destruction phase.

15:31.980 --> 15:34.900
The paper calls flaw number one, the missing numeraire.

15:34.900 --> 15:42.360
And when I was reading this section, my mind immediately went to the history books, specifically to the ghost of a guy named John Law.

15:42.960 --> 15:45.980
Oh, the historical parallel is absolutely unavoidable.

15:46.240 --> 15:47.940
And the paper fully acknowledges this.

15:48.600 --> 15:58.500
The mathematical void in the author's initial formula, you know, that C of X equals area times quality times location, is that the output is simply a weighted physical area.

15:58.800 --> 15:59.120
Right.

15:59.740 --> 16:02.720
It gives you a number of quality adjusted square meters.

16:02.720 --> 16:05.500
But what is the unit of account?

16:06.160 --> 16:13.360
How do you convert weighted square meters of compliant building into a circulating currency that people can use to buy groceries or pay their taxes?

16:13.720 --> 16:13.820
Right.

16:14.180 --> 16:26.820
If the government steps in and just arbitrarily sets a conversion rate, like say they declare, OK, one legally compliant square foot equals exactly $100 of new currency, then we haven't actually escaped fiat money at all.

16:26.940 --> 16:27.740
Not even a little bit.

16:27.740 --> 16:32.240
We just have fiat money with a really weird, highly specific construction rule attached to it.

16:32.540 --> 16:35.120
The government is still just making up the value out of thin air.

16:35.760 --> 16:45.360
And conversely, if you try to set the conversion rate by referencing the market price of floor space, like saying the currency unit is worth whatever a square foot currently sells for, congratulations.

16:45.900 --> 16:49.340
You've just invited the appraisal doom loop right back through the front door.

16:49.520 --> 16:50.480
It's inescapable.

16:50.740 --> 16:51.460
You haven't solved anything.

16:51.460 --> 16:56.020
You've just added a lot of administrative overhead to the exact same speculative problem.

16:56.860 --> 17:01.620
The paper defers this central question of what determines the price level initially.

17:02.320 --> 17:07.200
And the author admits deep into the analysis that this deferral is fatal to the concept of the currency.

17:07.200 --> 17:10.920
Which brings us back to John Law in 1705 France.

17:10.920 --> 17:18.920
For you listening, if you haven't brushed up on 18th century financial crises recently, Law proposed something strikingly similar in spirit.

17:19.480 --> 17:23.140
He basically wanted to issue paper money backed by the value of land.

17:23.520 --> 17:23.840
Yes.

17:24.400 --> 17:29.460
John Law looked at the French economy, which was struggling with a massive shortage of gold and silver coin,

17:29.460 --> 17:33.980
and reasoned that money issued against land would be perfectly secure.

17:34.600 --> 17:39.360
Because land is durable, it produces real wealth, and obviously it can't be stolen or moved.

17:39.800 --> 17:42.400
It's the ultimate stable collateral, or so he thought.

17:42.660 --> 17:43.100
Exactly.

17:43.480 --> 17:44.280
He thought it was perfect.

17:44.700 --> 17:51.100
But schemes like this, operating under the broader umbrella of what economic historians call the real bills doctrine,

17:51.720 --> 17:54.220
they always run into a catastrophic mismatch.

17:54.460 --> 17:58.920
Let's explore that mismatch, because I feel like it's the final nail in the coffin for Flaw one.

17:59.460 --> 18:05.100
If the money supply of an entire nation is rigidly tied to how fast we can pour concrete in frame houses,

18:05.420 --> 18:06.960
what happens to the rest of the economy?

18:07.560 --> 18:08.840
That's the billion dollar question.

18:09.140 --> 18:10.880
Imagine a massive technology boom.

18:11.240 --> 18:14.720
Suddenly, the economy is inventing new software, new digital services,

18:15.020 --> 18:18.520
and needs to make millions of rapid digital transactions every single day.

18:19.120 --> 18:24.100
The demand for money, just the basic need for liquid currency to facilitate trade, skyrockets.

18:24.280 --> 18:25.400
Right. It goes through the roof.

18:25.400 --> 18:31.020
But in this capacity-backed system, the money supply can't expand to facilitate those transactions,

18:31.280 --> 18:37.600
because we are literally waiting on a city inspector to drive his truck to a suburb and sign off on a drywall permit.

18:37.780 --> 18:39.120
That is the exact friction.

18:39.480 --> 18:46.640
The demand for money, the volume and velocity of transactions, an economy needs to settle, moves incredibly fast.

18:47.060 --> 18:51.920
It can change literally overnight based on technological shifts or even consumer behavior.

18:51.920 --> 18:55.400
But the physical stock of buildings is slow-moving.

18:55.880 --> 18:58.560
It takes years to plan, permit, and construct a building.

18:59.360 --> 19:04.620
Therefore, a slow-moving physical stock is a terrible match for a fast-moving transactional economy.

19:05.020 --> 19:05.860
That makes so much sense.

19:06.480 --> 19:10.640
Without a mechanism to dynamically connect the money supply to the actual demand for money,

19:11.340 --> 19:14.940
the system heavily trends toward a chronic scarcity of currency.

19:15.540 --> 19:17.580
You would experience severe deflation.

19:17.580 --> 19:19.520
Because money gets too rare.

19:19.980 --> 19:26.520
Exactly. Money becomes so rare, it gains value rapidly, causing people to hoard it and stop spending it entirely,

19:26.860 --> 19:30.200
simply because physical construction is a slow, laborious process.

19:30.420 --> 19:36.780
So flaw one basically proves you just can't use buildings as the literal printing press for a national currency.

19:37.020 --> 19:41.020
But flaw two is even trickier, I think, because it isn't just about math.

19:41.360 --> 19:43.720
It gets into some heavy political philosophy.

19:43.720 --> 19:47.720
The author calls it the non-separability of sight and improvement.

19:48.340 --> 19:51.540
Or, to put a historical name to it, the Henry George problem.

19:52.140 --> 19:55.740
This is such a classic, enduring dilemma in political economy.

19:56.280 --> 20:01.900
Henry George, writing in the late 19th century, made a very influential and compelling distinction.

20:02.620 --> 20:05.760
He divided real estate into two conceptual buckets.

20:06.260 --> 20:07.080
Okay, what are the buckets?

20:07.080 --> 20:12.320
The first is the value of the land itself, the scarce spatial privilege.

20:13.160 --> 20:17.600
The second is the value of the things built upon it, the certified improvement.

20:18.540 --> 20:21.860
George argued vehemently that we should heavily tax the land value,

20:21.980 --> 20:26.420
because that value is created collectively by the surrounding community and infrastructure.

20:26.640 --> 20:26.940
Right.

20:27.200 --> 20:29.860
But he argued we should not tax the improvements,

20:29.860 --> 20:33.940
because those are created by the private builder's labor and private investment.

20:34.460 --> 20:36.380
It makes a lot of intuitive sense, honestly.

20:36.380 --> 20:41.160
If I clear a lot and build a beautiful house with my own two hands, I created that value.

20:41.540 --> 20:45.540
But if the city built a park and a massive new school across the street,

20:45.700 --> 20:49.420
making my lot suddenly worth ten times more, I didn't create that value.

20:49.680 --> 20:50.500
The community did.

20:50.700 --> 20:51.080
Exactly.

20:51.340 --> 20:55.300
And the paper tries to apply this George's logic directly to monetary issuance.

20:55.660 --> 21:00.160
The author explicitly wants to issue money against the certified improvement, the house I built,

21:00.520 --> 21:05.160
but strictly prohibit issuing money against the scarce spatial privilege of the land.

21:05.160 --> 21:09.700
Conceptually, it's a really fascinating monetary application of Georgism.

21:10.460 --> 21:11.680
The intention is pure.

21:12.180 --> 21:14.740
Reward creation, don't reward passive holding.

21:15.380 --> 21:19.880
The problem, as the author painfully points out by stress testing their own math,

21:20.280 --> 21:25.940
is that practically and mathematically, the site and the improvement are not cleanly separable.

21:25.940 --> 21:31.320
Okay, let me set up a hypothetical to see if I understand this non-separability correctly.

21:31.720 --> 21:32.320
Go for it.

21:32.640 --> 21:35.020
Imagine two absolutely identical houses.

21:35.600 --> 21:40.100
Same blueprints, same exact materials, built by the exact same crew on the exact same day.

21:41.100 --> 21:47.340
House A is built next to a newly completed, massive, taxpayer-funded subway station in a booming city.

21:47.920 --> 21:51.900
House B is built next to an abandoned landfill three hours outside the city.

21:52.240 --> 21:52.900
Good contrast.

21:52.900 --> 21:58.820
Right. So, the physical capacity, the concrete, the wood, the code compliance, is exactly the same.

21:59.240 --> 22:03.800
But the economic reality is that House A will generate vastly more economic utility

22:03.800 --> 22:07.340
and command a vastly higher price simply because of where it is.

22:07.660 --> 22:08.180
Precisely.

22:08.660 --> 22:13.780
And let's look at the mathematical notation the author uses to explain why this completely breaks the system.

22:14.480 --> 22:18.120
Let the exchange value of a given parcel be represented by V of X.

22:18.560 --> 22:19.580
Okay, V for value.

22:19.580 --> 22:26.920
The naive assumption, the one the system really relies on, is that V of X simply equals the site value, which we'll call S,

22:27.360 --> 22:30.140
plus the separate improvement value, which we'll call I.

22:30.800 --> 22:32.640
Value equals site plus improvement.

22:33.000 --> 22:33.600
Sounds simple.

22:34.060 --> 22:34.520
Too simple.

22:34.900 --> 22:40.100
Because the complex reality is that V of X equals S plus I plus J.

22:40.500 --> 22:42.060
The dreaded J variable.

22:42.600 --> 22:43.160
What is J?

22:43.720 --> 22:45.320
J is the interaction term.

22:45.320 --> 22:47.120
It represents complementarity.

22:47.820 --> 22:53.240
It is the extent to which the value of the improvement depends on the specific site and vice versa.

22:53.500 --> 22:54.000
Oh, I see.

22:54.600 --> 23:01.480
Going back to your hypothetical, the upgraded transit connection next to House A raises the location coefficient of that parcel.

23:02.040 --> 23:05.000
But it also raises the utility of the house sitting on it.

23:05.540 --> 23:07.620
The house is more valuable because of the subway.

23:07.620 --> 23:13.180
And the subway is actually more valuable because there are houses next to it to provide daily riders.

23:13.620 --> 23:15.320
They constantly interact with each other.

23:15.500 --> 23:15.760
They do.

23:16.340 --> 23:24.600
If you issue money against that increment of improvement, even if you claim you are only valuing the house, you are, in effect, capturing a public investment.

23:25.280 --> 23:30.000
You're monetizing the J term, which was created by the taxpayers who funded the subway.

23:30.000 --> 23:34.060
It's like a massive wealth transfer hiding inside a math equation.

23:34.420 --> 23:36.880
Now, I want to make a really quick note for you listening.

23:37.360 --> 23:43.100
We are neutrally describing this Georgist convention purely as a policy choice within the context of the paper.

23:43.760 --> 23:50.580
We aren't getting into the politics of whether taxing land and ignoring structures or vice versa is the right or wrong way to run a society.

23:50.980 --> 23:51.220
Absolutely not.

23:51.220 --> 23:57.880
That is fundamentally a massive political decision about who captures the economic gains of that J interaction term.

23:58.180 --> 24:01.640
We aren't endorsing it one way or the other, and frankly, neither is the paper.

24:01.900 --> 24:09.260
The author is simply pointing out that this system forces you to make a rigid convention, an unavoidable rule about who gets the money from J.

24:09.500 --> 24:09.900
Exactly.

24:10.340 --> 24:13.040
It's not a natural, objective, physical measurement.

24:13.540 --> 24:18.440
It's a highly subjective policy choice dressed up as objective physics.

24:19.100 --> 24:20.140
Which is dangerous.

24:20.140 --> 24:25.000
Which is exactly why the author concludes that the formulation fails at this stage.

24:25.620 --> 24:26.380
Think about it this way.

24:27.220 --> 24:36.560
Aggregate land rent in a city, under idealized economic conditions, is generally just enough to finance the public goods that make the land valuable in the first place.

24:37.120 --> 24:39.380
Like the roads, the sewers, the police, the schools.

24:39.740 --> 24:40.000
Right.

24:40.000 --> 24:52.080
If a monetary system refuses to issue against site value to avoid speculation, it is basically refusing to issue against the capitalized value of the infrastructure the community already paid for.

24:52.080 --> 25:03.820
It is a coherent policy position to take, certainly, but it absolutely destroys the illusion that the money being created is just a neutral, apolitical reflection of physical capacity.

25:03.820 --> 25:08.840
It completely forces the monetary system to take a controversial political stance.

25:08.840 --> 25:17.200
So, we have the missing numeraire causing massive deflation, and the Henry George problem forcing political choices into the math.

25:17.600 --> 25:22.680
But if flaws one and two weren't enough to sink the ship, flaw three is the absolute killer.

25:22.800 --> 25:27.300
I have to say, when I read this part, I literally laughed out loud because it's just such a human problem.

25:27.300 --> 25:28.440
Oh, it really is.

25:28.620 --> 25:32.180
It is the most severe internal contradiction in the whole paper.

25:32.480 --> 25:35.840
Flaw three is zoning as money printing.

25:36.100 --> 25:36.860
It's so funny.

25:37.480 --> 25:38.900
It's a devastating realization.

25:39.500 --> 25:42.040
Let's go back to the fundamental formula one more time.

25:42.940 --> 25:46.100
C of X equals A of X times Q of X times L of X.

25:46.500 --> 25:50.160
And remember, A is the legally permitted usable floor area.

25:50.320 --> 25:51.320
Legally permitted.

25:51.720 --> 25:53.720
That is the trap right there.

25:53.720 --> 26:03.720
If they measure the legally permitted floor area, I mean, what's stopping a town from just literally changing the definition of what's legal?

26:04.180 --> 26:06.040
That is exactly the loophole.

26:06.420 --> 26:11.660
Because zoning designations and permitted floor area ratios are not physical facts of the universe.

26:12.540 --> 26:14.600
They are instruments of public policy.

26:15.060 --> 26:19.500
They are legislative variables determined by city councils and zoning boards.

26:19.900 --> 26:20.140
Yeah.

26:20.140 --> 26:31.060
If a municipality simply votes on a random Tuesday night to change the zoning laws to allow 50-story skyscrapers on a city block that used to only allow single-family homes,

26:31.500 --> 26:37.100
they have mathematically exponentially increased the variable A for all those parcels.

26:37.440 --> 26:38.900
Without laying a single brick.

26:39.700 --> 26:40.640
They didn't pour any concrete.

26:41.160 --> 26:42.420
They didn't hire a single carpenter.

26:42.940 --> 26:44.680
They didn't even dig a hole in the ground.

26:44.900 --> 26:48.400
They just changed the legal state space on a piece of paper.

26:48.400 --> 26:57.580
And under the original rules of this system, where money is issued against capacity, that city council just literally printed money out of thin air.

26:58.040 --> 26:58.440
Yes, they did.

26:59.480 --> 27:03.980
The paper's golden rule was nothing physically changed, therefore nothing monetary changed.

27:04.080 --> 27:11.280
But here, a purely legal zoning amendment creates massive monetary capacity without a single physical change actually occurring.

27:11.280 --> 27:15.780
The political incentives this would create would be catastrophic and completely corrupting.

27:16.140 --> 27:23.600
A town that wanted to expand its local money supply or, you know, just enrich its local property owners could just infinitely redraw its zoning maps.

27:23.780 --> 27:24.680
People would go crazy.

27:25.080 --> 27:30.940
Property owners would lobby City Hall endlessly for upzoning, not because they actually intend to ever build a skyscraper,

27:31.400 --> 27:36.400
but simply to acquire a claim on the newly issued currency that the upzoning magically generates.

27:36.400 --> 27:40.220
It reminds me of the classic economic principle of Goodhart's law.

27:40.940 --> 27:41.640
You know, the one.

27:41.840 --> 27:44.480
When a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure.

27:44.960 --> 27:51.700
Oh, the paper explicitly cites Goodhart's law in this section, along with Campbell and Stratherer's work on metric fixation.

27:52.020 --> 27:54.680
It's just a textbook vulnerability in system design.

27:54.680 --> 27:57.220
It's exactly like teaching to the test.

27:57.440 --> 28:03.220
If you tell a school district that you are going to reward teachers based entirely on standardized test scores, what happens?

28:03.720 --> 28:08.460
The teachers stop teaching critical thinking, they stop teaching art, they stop fostering curiosity,

28:08.460 --> 28:12.560
and they just drill multiple choice test answers into the kids' heads all day long.

28:12.620 --> 28:12.720
Yep.

28:12.940 --> 28:14.060
They optimize for the metric.

28:14.340 --> 28:14.660
Exactly.

28:15.080 --> 28:23.060
In this capacity-backed system, builders are going to immediately optimize for the cheapest possible floor area that legally qualifies for the money.

28:23.060 --> 28:31.020
If the formula rewards square footage and basic code compliance, they will completely ignore all the hidden qualities of a building that actually make it good.

28:32.020 --> 28:33.580
They'll use the cheapest legal materials.

28:34.040 --> 28:39.980
They won't care about the durability of concealed pipes or the quality of finishes or the aesthetic impact on the neighborhood

28:39.980 --> 28:43.860
because those things aren't on the inspector's checklist for monetary issuance.

28:44.220 --> 28:47.100
The developer's objective mathematically shifts.

28:47.100 --> 28:55.660
Instead of maximizing the actual utility, beauty, and social value of the building for future tenants, they just maximize credit eligibility.

28:56.260 --> 28:59.460
They are building to satisfy an algorithm, not a human need.

28:59.840 --> 29:05.420
And this fundamentally changes the role of the building inspector, which is perhaps the most dangerous side effect of the whole thing.

29:05.840 --> 29:09.940
Right, because right now a building inspector is mostly there to just make sure my deck doesn't collapse.

29:09.940 --> 29:14.720
But in this system, the inspector literally becomes the gatekeeper to newly minted money.

29:14.980 --> 29:16.340
They become an adversary.

29:17.200 --> 29:17.480
Exactly.

29:17.960 --> 29:21.660
The inspector functions as what computer science calls an oracle.

29:22.360 --> 29:26.860
The financial ledger, the database tracking all this money, is closed and mathematical.

29:27.580 --> 29:33.100
But it has to trust an off-ledger, real-world observation made by a flawed human being.

29:33.220 --> 29:34.020
That's a lot of pressure.

29:34.020 --> 29:44.040
The integrity of the entire national monetary system would be bounded mathematically by the integrity of that single inspector's observation on a Tuesday morning.

29:44.820 --> 29:50.060
So, to prevent corruption, you suddenly need massive, heavy-handed adversarial institutions.

29:50.680 --> 29:51.500
Like what?

29:51.880 --> 29:57.220
You would need constant randomized rotation of inspectors so developers couldn't buddy up with them.

29:57.860 --> 30:01.080
You'd need unpredictable sampling audits by federal overseers.

30:01.080 --> 30:07.040
You'd need intense legal liability, maybe even criminal penalties for false attestations.

30:07.820 --> 30:14.540
And you'd need a really robust legal mechanism to challenge and reverse credit issuance if fraud is discovered years later.

30:14.940 --> 30:22.620
You know, listening to all this talk of ledgers and trusting oracles and cryptographic records, my mind immediately goes to Web3.

30:22.620 --> 30:31.860
I can easily imagine a listener right now thinking, wait a minute, is this entire 2026 paper just an elaborate pitch for a real estate cryptocurrency?

30:32.560 --> 30:35.580
Is this literally just blockchain for a drywall?

30:35.800 --> 30:40.000
It is a very natural assumption to make, given the vocabulary the author uses.

30:40.500 --> 30:45.240
They use terms like append-only records, hashing, and decentralized verification.

30:45.240 --> 30:54.000
But the author devotes a significant, highly technical section of the paper to fiercely and explicitly rejecting that comparison.

30:54.420 --> 30:55.380
Okay, good to know.

30:55.980 --> 31:04.940
The architecture of this system might share some superficial structural similarities with crypto, but they are built to solve entirely different, almost opposite problems.

31:05.380 --> 31:09.980
Let's dive into that distinction because I think it clarifies a lot about what makes this paper so unique.

31:10.620 --> 31:14.180
Why is this emphatically not a blockchain?

31:14.180 --> 31:20.100
The fundamental difference lies in the concept of verification and what is actually being verified.

31:20.920 --> 31:25.420
Blockchains, at their core, solve the problem of agreement about records.

31:25.740 --> 31:26.060
Agreement.

31:26.660 --> 31:26.960
Yes.

31:27.380 --> 31:31.860
If you have 10,000 computers distributed around the world, you have a massive problem.

31:32.360 --> 31:35.620
How do they all agree on who owns what without a central bank?

31:35.620 --> 31:47.180
A consensus mechanism, like proof-of-work and Bitcoin or proof-of-stake, ensures that all 10,000 computers agree on which history of the ledger is the canonical, true history.

31:47.580 --> 31:47.780
Right.

31:48.300 --> 31:50.940
It basically stops the double-spend problem digitally.

31:51.460 --> 31:59.420
It makes sure I can't send the same digital token to two different people, which is, you know, an incredible computer science breakthrough for digital scarcity.

31:59.420 --> 32:00.360
Yes, it is.

32:00.580 --> 32:04.260
But this built capacity system doesn't care primarily about digital agreement.

32:04.960 --> 32:08.140
It must solve the problem of warrant for records.

32:09.140 --> 32:12.680
Consensus protocols can agree perfectly that a digital certificate was signed.

32:13.500 --> 32:16.320
10,000 nodes can easily verify the cryptography.

32:16.740 --> 32:22.520
But a blockchain cannot verify if the foundation was actually reinforced in physical reality.

32:22.520 --> 32:24.500
Oh, that makes so much sense.

32:24.560 --> 32:25.260
It's like Wikipedia.

32:26.320 --> 32:26.920
Oh, how so?

32:27.460 --> 32:43.200
While the servers hosting Wikipedia are perfectly secure, the code running the site is totally flawless, the database replicates across the world instantly, but if the human being typing the article is lying, the flawless code just beautifully, immutably preserves a lie.

32:43.580 --> 32:44.080
Exactly.

32:44.660 --> 32:49.300
I could get 10,000 decentralized computers to agree perfectly on a total falsehood.

32:49.300 --> 32:56.220
If the builder bribed the building inspector, the blockchain just faithfully records the bribed lie forever.

32:56.920 --> 33:01.340
The real vulnerability in the system isn't some hacker breaking the cryptography in a basement.

33:01.800 --> 33:06.100
The vulnerability is literally a guy with a clipboard taking a bribe on a construction site.

33:06.380 --> 33:09.060
That Wikipedia analogy is so spot on.

33:09.940 --> 33:13.860
The paper states this beautifully by defining what they call the trust boundary.

33:13.860 --> 33:20.540
In a blockchain network, the trust boundary is purely internal, like which history of the digital ledger do we accept as valid?

33:20.940 --> 33:21.060
Right.

33:21.280 --> 33:25.660
But in the capacity architecture, the trust boundary is the physical oracle itself.

33:26.400 --> 33:31.360
The fundamental question is, did this physical event actually happen in the dirt and the concrete?

33:32.340 --> 33:36.560
Replicating a false attestation across 10,000 nodes makes the record highly durable.

33:36.940 --> 33:41.080
It makes it impossible to delete, but it absolutely does not make it true.

33:41.080 --> 33:47.400
And this brings up a huge philosophical difference about a word the crypto community just loves, immutability.

33:48.080 --> 33:55.720
The fact that you cannot change, edit, or delete a record once it's on the blockchain is always touted as its greatest feature.

33:55.900 --> 33:56.720
It's permanent.

33:57.400 --> 34:01.920
Yes, but in the physical world, immutability is a bug, not a feature.

34:02.360 --> 34:02.720
Right.

34:02.720 --> 34:06.540
The physical world is messy, degradable, and subject to error.

34:07.400 --> 34:11.760
What happens if a building that was issued money burns to the ground the very next week?

34:12.560 --> 34:22.580
What happens if you discover three years later during a renovation that the original inspector was indeed bribed and the required fire separations simply don't exist behind the drywall?

34:22.580 --> 34:30.360
You can't just pretend the building is still there or that the fire separations magically exist just because the blockchain says it's written in stone.

34:30.580 --> 34:34.600
You have to be able to correct the record to reflect physical reality.

34:35.380 --> 34:35.820
Precisely.

34:36.520 --> 34:41.320
An immutable record is not necessarily an accurate record of a mutable world.

34:42.000 --> 34:46.520
The system requires what the author calls a defeating entry or a retraction.

34:46.520 --> 34:50.020
How does a retraction work without deleting history?

34:50.860 --> 35:01.480
Well, importantly, you don't silently delete the old fraudulent record because full auditability requires an append-only history to track exactly who made the mistake or committed the fraud.

35:02.180 --> 35:07.780
Instead, you add a new entry to the chain that explicitly retracts the authority of the previous one.

35:08.300 --> 35:09.060
Oh, okay.

35:09.060 --> 35:16.400
The historical ledger preserves the error for auditing purposes, but the authoritative present state reflects the correction.

35:16.840 --> 35:19.760
It basically aligns the ledger back with physical truth.

35:20.260 --> 35:21.060
That makes total sense.

35:21.420 --> 35:24.180
It's a ledger of reality, not a ledger of algorithms.

35:24.880 --> 35:29.540
And another point the paper makes in rejecting the crypto comparison is about scarcity itself.

35:29.540 --> 35:39.420
Bitcoin uses artificial computational scarcity, literally wasting massive amounts of electricity solving math puzzles to regulate consensus and limit supply.

35:40.100 --> 35:45.800
Proof of stake uses artificial protocol asset scarcity, limiting supply based on who holds the most tokens.

35:46.320 --> 35:46.440
Right.

35:47.180 --> 35:52.180
And this built capacity system has absolutely no need to manufacture artificial scarcity through math.

35:52.820 --> 35:58.520
The constraints on the money supply are already perfectly provided by physical reality and human institutions.

35:58.520 --> 36:00.300
Because land is finite.

36:00.720 --> 36:00.980
Exactly.

36:01.400 --> 36:04.160
Land occupies finite Euclidean locations.

36:04.880 --> 36:10.180
Construction consumes finite physical materials, lumber, steel, copper, and finite human labor.

36:11.080 --> 36:13.440
Physical transformations take actual time to complete.

36:14.260 --> 36:15.620
Inspections take human effort.

36:16.300 --> 36:19.180
The physical world is just inherently stubbornly scarce.

36:19.860 --> 36:23.740
You don't need to invent a cryptographic math puzzle to slow the inflation rate down.

36:23.740 --> 36:28.320
The speed of pouring concrete and framing timber naturally regulates the pace of issuance.

36:28.520 --> 36:29.900
It's self-regulating in that way.

36:30.360 --> 36:32.540
Okay, let's take a breath and recap where we are.

36:33.080 --> 36:34.040
It's not a blockchain.

36:35.200 --> 36:43.880
And the literal idea of capacity-backed money, treating buildings as literal currency, fails catastrophically because of the missing numeraire, causing deflation.

36:44.340 --> 36:47.580
The Henry George problem, forcing political wealth transfers.

36:47.880 --> 36:49.940
And the zoning loophole, causing Goodhart's Law.

36:50.300 --> 36:52.540
The author thoroughly tore down their own house.

36:53.080 --> 36:53.700
They really did.

36:54.260 --> 36:56.080
So, what are we actually left with?

36:56.560 --> 37:02.460
How do we save the core, compelling insight that value should be tied to physical truth rather than market speculation?

37:03.080 --> 37:05.420
This is where we reach the major pivot in the paper.

37:06.100 --> 37:10.220
We transition from a theory of money to a theory of credit.

37:10.780 --> 37:11.580
Okay, credit.

37:11.580 --> 37:16.740
And to understand why this pivot isn't just a slight adjustment, but a strictly necessary overhaul.

37:17.200 --> 37:20.900
We really have to talk about what the author calls the burning building problem.

37:21.540 --> 37:22.820
The burning building problem.

37:22.820 --> 37:26.100
That sounds like the ultimate stress test of the physical money theory.

37:26.300 --> 37:26.700
Oh, yes.

37:26.700 --> 37:29.540
Let's say we stubbornly stuck with the original idea.

37:30.120 --> 37:36.800
The total monetary base of the national economy is directly rigidly tied to the total stock of physical build capacity.

37:37.360 --> 37:41.980
Every building in the country represents a fraction of the circulating currency in people's wallets.

37:42.280 --> 37:43.120
Okay, I'm with you.

37:43.340 --> 37:47.480
Now, imagine a massive warehouse in Ohio burns to the ground.

37:47.920 --> 37:51.180
Or a hurricane wipes out a coastal town in Florida.

37:51.180 --> 37:56.120
Massive amounts of physical capacity are destroyed literally overnight.

37:56.920 --> 38:00.460
If the physical capacity drops, the monetary base must drop to match it.

38:00.780 --> 38:04.920
Otherwise, the money is no longer fully backed by capacity and the whole premise is just a lie.

38:05.240 --> 38:05.600
Exactly.

38:06.280 --> 38:08.640
The math demands a reduction in the money supply.

38:09.460 --> 38:12.180
But how do you actually execute that in the real world?

38:12.780 --> 38:13.880
Oh, right.

38:14.220 --> 38:18.120
The money issued against that Florida town has already escaped into general circulation.

38:18.640 --> 38:20.260
It's sitting in bank accounts in New York.

38:20.260 --> 38:22.480
It's being used to buy groceries in Texas.

38:22.860 --> 38:29.780
You can't just randomly delete thousands of dollars from your checking account in California because a warehouse in Ohio burned down.

38:30.420 --> 38:31.640
That would be insane.

38:32.660 --> 38:33.840
I didn't cause the fire.

38:34.000 --> 38:37.860
I have absolutely no relationship to the warehouse, and I don't even know who owns it.

38:38.020 --> 38:38.240
Right.

38:38.500 --> 38:41.180
That would cause an immediate catastrophic bank run.

38:41.440 --> 38:49.800
If normal people knew their life savings could just literally vanish from their accounts at any moment because of a localized fire or flood three states over, they would panic.

38:50.260 --> 38:52.400
They would pull all their money out of the system immediately.

38:52.800 --> 38:53.100
Obviously.

38:53.580 --> 39:00.900
The alternative is that the government would have to step in and basically lower the backing ratio, saying, okay, money is now only 90% backed by buildings.

39:01.200 --> 39:06.680
Which completely admits the whole fully backed thing was a fiction anyway, bringing us right back to fiat money.

39:06.680 --> 39:07.200
Exactly.

39:07.680 --> 39:13.740
The paper actually includes another incredibly detailed dimensionless simulation to illustrate exactly this.

39:14.220 --> 39:17.060
They call it the capacity destruction shock.

39:17.640 --> 39:18.860
And what does the simulation show?

39:18.860 --> 39:26.200
It graphs a literal stock-backed monetary base crashing down universally when a discrete localized loss event occurs.

39:27.020 --> 39:37.760
The simulation shows that the incidence of the loss falls on every single holder of the currency across the entire economy, entirely irrespective of their actual exposure to the physical asset that was destroyed.

39:37.900 --> 39:38.500
Wow.

39:38.500 --> 39:46.620
It essentially proves mathematically that a currency tied to a physical stock is completely unworkable in a world where things break, burn, and degrade.

39:46.840 --> 39:49.460
So the currency idea is officially dead.

39:50.000 --> 39:51.080
What is the author's solution?

39:51.660 --> 39:54.920
The solution is this central conceptual leap of the entire paper.

39:54.920 --> 39:59.520
The author basically writes, stop treating the stock of buildings as money.

40:00.220 --> 40:05.420
Treat the event of a certified building transformation as underwriting information for a loan.

40:05.420 --> 40:09.840
Okay, let me chew on that and try an analogy to make sure I'm really grasping the shift here.

40:10.040 --> 40:10.220
Go ahead.

40:10.480 --> 40:18.820
It's the difference between looking at a newly finished house and saying this house is a million dollars, which creates all those destruction problems we just talked about,

40:18.820 --> 40:25.780
versus saying the act of you building this house proves you have done something objectively valuable in the real world.

40:26.300 --> 40:34.160
Therefore, based on that verified physical act, you are granted a million-dollar loan, which you have to pay back over time as the house physically ages.

40:35.020 --> 40:36.200
That is exactly the mechanism.

40:36.620 --> 40:41.860
You shift from issuing a permanent currency to originating an amortizing transformation loan.

40:42.280 --> 40:46.320
If a building burns down now, it absolutely does not destroy the national money supply.

40:46.980 --> 40:48.260
Because it's just a loan.

40:48.260 --> 40:48.780
Right.

40:49.160 --> 40:55.160
It simply destroys the collateral for a specific loan held by a specific borrower and a specific lending institution,

40:55.680 --> 40:58.660
who willingly and explicitly contracted for that exact exposure.

40:59.460 --> 41:01.840
The systemic contagion is completely contained.

41:02.320 --> 41:03.400
That makes total sense.

41:03.640 --> 41:07.980
If that Florida town washes away, the local banks and insurers take a massive hit.

41:08.200 --> 41:12.140
But the checking account of a teacher in California remains perfectly untouched.

41:12.520 --> 41:15.860
It localizes the destruction to the parties who actually took the risk.

41:16.040 --> 41:17.980
That solves the burning building problem.

41:18.260 --> 41:18.940
Perfectly.

41:19.160 --> 41:20.240
It really does.

41:20.920 --> 41:26.800
And crucially, it also naturally accounts for depreciation, which the currency model really struggled with.

41:27.560 --> 41:29.740
Buildings naturally decay over time, right?

41:30.200 --> 41:32.300
Roofs leak, foundations settle, pipes corrode.

41:32.720 --> 41:33.600
Things fall apart.

41:33.600 --> 41:44.420
In a loan model, the loan naturally amortizes, meaning it is paid down by the borrower month by month at a rate that is designed to at least match the physical depreciation of the asset.

41:44.420 --> 41:45.860
Oh, that's clever.

41:46.280 --> 41:49.900
And if the homeowner does maintenance, like if they put on a new roof?

41:49.900 --> 41:56.520
That constitutes a new certified physical transformation that can generate a new credit increment to offset the decay.

41:57.020 --> 41:59.760
It mirrors the physical life cycle of the building perfectly.

41:59.760 --> 42:02.660
But wait, I see a potential hole here.

42:02.740 --> 42:07.060
We transition to loans, which completely fixes the destruction problem.

42:07.560 --> 42:11.800
But we still have the Goodhart's Law problem and the zoning loophole and the Henry George problem.

42:11.800 --> 42:20.500
If the size of the loan is based on the value of the building, aren't we still just guessing what it's worth or letting city councils print money by upzoning?

42:20.800 --> 42:23.860
We absolutely would be if the loan was based on market value.

42:24.520 --> 42:29.380
But the author introduces the final crucial piece of the puzzle to lock the system down completely.

42:30.040 --> 42:31.760
The replacement cost index.

42:31.960 --> 42:33.360
The replacement cost index.

42:33.360 --> 42:33.800
Yes.

42:34.260 --> 42:48.620
To prevent speculative bidding and political manipulation from creeping back in, the permitted credit for a transformation is based strictly and exclusively on the standardized regional replacement cost of the physical materials and labor required to actually build it.

42:48.740 --> 42:49.260
Oh, I see.

42:49.320 --> 42:53.020
So if I want to refinance my house under this system, we aren't looking at Zillow.

42:53.240 --> 42:56.160
We aren't looking at recent sales of similar homes in the area.

42:56.280 --> 42:56.960
No Zillow.

42:56.960 --> 43:09.580
The bank looks at a localized, heavily audited index of what drywall, two-by-fours, copper piping, concrete, and union labor actually cost to procure in my specific zip code on that specific day.

43:09.660 --> 43:10.140
Precisely.

43:10.680 --> 43:15.540
The credit is tethered to the physical cost of production, not the subjective price of exchange.

43:16.360 --> 43:21.580
Let's look at how this officially blocks that massive zoning-as-money-printing loophole we discussed earlier.

43:21.680 --> 43:22.680
Yeah, how does it fix that?

43:22.680 --> 43:26.080
The rule is basically reformulated under this credit paradigm.

43:26.820 --> 43:30.100
No realized physical transformation equals no credit.

43:30.900 --> 43:43.300
If the city council votes to upzone a vacant lot to allow a 50-story skyscraper, but the owner hasn't actually poured any concrete or bought any steel, the fiscal replacement cost of their transformation is literally zero.

43:43.420 --> 43:44.900
Because they haven't built anything yet?

43:45.140 --> 43:48.000
Therefore, their credit eligibility is exactly zero.

43:48.000 --> 43:55.280
They cannot lobby the city council to print money for them, because the legal right to build is not a physical transformation.

43:55.860 --> 43:56.740
That is just brilliant.

43:57.280 --> 44:03.440
The legislative change, the upzoning, just creates an option, an entitlement to build in the future.

44:04.060 --> 44:11.300
But the built, certified physical capacity measured in raw materials and labor is the absolute only base for credit.

44:11.300 --> 44:14.380
And it neatly solves the Henry George problem as well.

44:15.220 --> 44:23.180
By strictly tying the loan amount to the replacement cost of the physical structure, you are mathematically excluding the speculative scarcity rent of the site itself.

44:23.580 --> 44:27.080
Let's go back to my hypothetical of the two identical houses to test that.

44:27.360 --> 44:28.740
House A, next to the subway.

44:29.140 --> 44:30.600
House B, next to the landfill.

44:30.600 --> 44:36.380
Because they are physically identical, they require the exact same amount of wood, concrete, and labor to build.

44:36.920 --> 44:41.900
Therefore, under the Replacement Cost Index, they qualify for the exact same amount of credit.

44:42.380 --> 44:46.060
Even though House A would sell for 10 times more on the open market.

44:46.440 --> 44:46.840
Exactly.

44:47.460 --> 44:50.640
The system just flat out refuses to monetize the location premium.

44:50.640 --> 44:57.760
Now, a house built in a remote northern town might qualify for a slightly different loan amount than an identical house in a southern city,

44:57.980 --> 45:06.460
but only because the raw cost of transporting materials and hiring specialized labor to work in the freezing cold north genuinely costs more in physical terms.

45:06.980 --> 45:11.700
It has absolutely nothing to do with the fact that the southern city's land is considered trendier.

45:11.940 --> 45:18.060
It fundamentally separates the physical conditions of production from the speculative scarcity of space.

45:18.060 --> 45:26.520
It forces the financial ledger to finally tell the objective truth about the physical world without getting distracted by market psychology.

45:26.780 --> 45:29.880
It does. It anchors finance in physics and labor.

45:30.560 --> 45:36.340
But we really have to address the geography aspect more closely because the author originally had a very radical,

45:36.920 --> 45:41.160
almost sci-fi idea about space and money that they ultimately had to abandon.

45:41.500 --> 45:43.020
Right, the spatial currency idea?

45:43.120 --> 45:46.420
Yes. I want to talk about this because it's such a wild thought experiment.

45:46.420 --> 45:50.860
The paper originally proposed what it called a spatial currency.

45:51.400 --> 46:00.340
The idea was that a dollar originated by a physical building should actually be worth less the further it travels geographically from that specific building.

46:00.680 --> 46:05.260
Yes. The mathematical concept the author toyed with was a symmetric penalty for distance.

46:05.840 --> 46:08.100
They used an exponential decay function to model it.

46:08.780 --> 46:11.280
How does an exponential decay function work for money?

46:11.280 --> 46:18.860
Imagine a vast graph where the vertices are municipalities and the edges connecting them are physical infrastructure like roads or rail lines.

46:19.920 --> 46:29.960
A unit of currency issued at location U would exchange for a unit at location V at a rate that decays exponentially based on the infrastructural distance between them.

46:29.960 --> 46:32.200
So, let me put myself in this world.

46:32.620 --> 46:37.920
If I get a loan issued against my newly built house in Boston, that money is perfectly strong.

46:38.220 --> 46:41.580
It's worth 100 cents on the dollar as long as I spend it in Boston.

46:41.780 --> 46:42.040
Correct.

46:42.380 --> 46:48.180
But if I take a flight and try to spend that exact same money in Seattle, the system automatically haircuts it?

46:48.540 --> 46:52.340
It's mathematically worth less because it's far away from its collateral.

46:52.340 --> 46:54.240
That was the initial thought experiment, yeah.

46:54.640 --> 46:56.940
And the math inside the paper actually holds up internally.

46:57.540 --> 47:01.040
The author provides proofs showing there is no routing arbitrage.

47:01.440 --> 47:02.080
What does that mean?

47:02.520 --> 47:08.800
Meaning you can't game the system by bouncing the money through 10 intermediate cities to artificially preserve its value.

47:09.740 --> 47:14.520
The shortest infrastructural path is always the most efficient exchange rate, but...

47:14.520 --> 47:18.840
The math might work on a chalkboard, but human behavior absolutely ruins it.

47:18.900 --> 47:19.460
It always does.

47:19.460 --> 47:25.740
If I live in this system and I have two types of money in my digital wallet, one that holds its value everywhere I go,

47:26.160 --> 47:32.460
and one that loses, say, 10% of its value if I drive across town, I know exactly what I'm doing.

47:32.620 --> 47:37.280
I am spending the weak local money as fast as humanly possible trying to get rid of it,

47:37.360 --> 47:41.840
and I am hoarding the strong global money under my mattress or in a vault somewhere.

47:42.100 --> 47:44.200
You've just perfectly described Gresham's Law.

47:44.560 --> 47:46.900
It's literally one of the oldest principles in economics.

47:47.240 --> 47:49.040
Bad money drives out good.

47:49.460 --> 47:56.880
If you have two currencies circulating side by side, people will always spend the one they perceive as weaker and hoard the one they perceive as stronger.

47:57.180 --> 47:58.120
That's just human nature.

47:58.860 --> 48:02.460
The paper totally acknowledges this fatal flaw in the spatial currency idea.

48:02.460 --> 48:10.080
If a booming region is building a lot and issuing lots of this local spatial currency and a neighboring stagnant region isn't,

48:10.560 --> 48:14.120
the fixed decay conversion rate will cause absolute economic chaos.

48:14.520 --> 48:15.040
Chaos how?

48:15.040 --> 48:20.020
Goods will flood into the boom region to capture the local currency before it decays.

48:20.620 --> 48:26.180
People will hoard the scarce currency of the stagnant region, and you'll have a massive, unmanageable economic distortion.

48:26.180 --> 48:32.240
The paper actually mentions a couple of fascinating historical precedents here to prove why this doesn't work, right?

48:32.340 --> 48:36.680
Like the Swiss Weir network and the Virgil experiment?

48:37.360 --> 48:37.560
Yes.

48:37.840 --> 48:42.720
Those two examples are almost always brought up by proponents of alternative or local currencies.

48:43.500 --> 48:49.040
The Swiss Weir is a firm-to-firm mutual credit network that has existed since the 1930s.

48:49.040 --> 48:51.620
But it is fundamentally counter-cyclical.

48:52.080 --> 48:57.660
It expands when the normal banking system contracts and is not physically backed in the way this paper proposes.

48:58.460 --> 48:59.160
And what about Virgil?

48:59.700 --> 49:02.560
The more relevant example is the Virgil experiment.

49:03.380 --> 49:07.020
Virgil was a small Austrian town in the 1930s during the Great Depression.

49:07.680 --> 49:11.100
The mayor, who was basically desperate to stimulate the local economy,

49:11.720 --> 49:16.660
experimented with an idea from a theorist named Silvio Gazelle called demurage-bearing money.

49:17.400 --> 49:17.880
Demurage.

49:17.880 --> 49:20.900
That means money that essentially rusts, right?

49:21.100 --> 49:21.440
Exactly.

49:22.080 --> 49:24.240
The Virgil currency literally decayed over time.

49:25.120 --> 49:29.600
To keep a banknote valid, you had to physically buy a stamp every month and affix it to the note.

49:29.980 --> 49:31.140
That is wild.

49:31.520 --> 49:33.200
It functioned as a penalty for hoarding.

49:33.660 --> 49:35.740
It forced people to spend the money instantly,

49:36.080 --> 49:40.700
which rapidly increased the velocity of money and temporarily stimulated the town's economy,

49:41.140 --> 49:43.480
getting people back to work, paving roads, and building bridges.

49:43.880 --> 49:45.320
It's a fascinating piece of history.

49:45.320 --> 49:49.920
But the author realizes that these historical examples, while super interesting,

49:50.380 --> 49:53.360
don't actually support a built capacity backing rule.

49:53.680 --> 49:58.040
The Gresham dynamic, the tendency for people to game the differing values of money,

49:58.180 --> 50:00.080
is simply too strong to overcome.

50:00.340 --> 50:00.460
Right.

50:00.460 --> 50:04.940
So, facing this reality, the author makes another brilliant pivot.

50:05.380 --> 50:07.300
They demote the spatial element.

50:07.740 --> 50:08.140
They do.

50:08.500 --> 50:09.400
Demote it how?

50:09.700 --> 50:11.720
Do they just give up on geography entirely?

50:12.020 --> 50:12.820
Not entirely, no.

50:13.380 --> 50:15.760
They abandon the idea of a decaying currency.

50:16.400 --> 50:19.680
We don't need a new, exotic, geographically constrained money.

50:19.680 --> 50:24.580
We just use normal dollars or euros or whatever the national fiat currency is.

50:25.180 --> 50:29.040
But the ledger tracking the loans retains the spatial topology.

50:29.720 --> 50:30.560
Wait, I don't follow.

50:30.960 --> 50:35.860
We use the graph of vertices and municipalities and roads not to punish the currency unit,

50:36.240 --> 50:39.440
but to intelligently manage systemic risk behind the scenes.

50:40.140 --> 50:40.700
Oh, I see.

50:40.700 --> 50:45.880
So, a local bank is issuing perfectly normal USD loans to builders against these certified

50:45.880 --> 50:46.960
physical improvements.

50:47.660 --> 50:49.720
The money spends exactly the same everywhere.

50:50.260 --> 50:55.720
But the central ledger, or maybe the regulator, can see exactly where all that physical collateral

50:55.720 --> 50:57.240
physically sits on the map.

50:57.320 --> 50:57.560
Yes.

50:58.180 --> 51:00.560
You define a prudential risk rule based on the geography.

51:01.180 --> 51:02.640
A regulator can say to a bank,

51:02.640 --> 51:07.720
Looking at the spatial ledger, we see that 40% of your collateral is backed by buildings

51:07.720 --> 51:10.040
that rely on a single municipal water system.

51:10.700 --> 51:12.440
That is way too much correlated risk.

51:12.800 --> 51:13.680
You must diversify.

51:14.000 --> 51:15.040
That is so smart.

51:15.600 --> 51:20.540
Or they could say, no more than 5% of your total loan portfolio can be located in this

51:20.540 --> 51:22.160
specific coastal floodplain.

51:22.620 --> 51:25.700
Because if a hurricane hits, your collateral is completely wiped out.

51:26.540 --> 51:32.200
The spatial topology basically becomes a sophisticated tool for auditing systemic infrastructure risk,

51:32.200 --> 51:36.600
rather than a weird penalty on the unit of account that consumers have to deal with.

51:37.440 --> 51:38.720
That is incredibly practical.

51:39.620 --> 51:45.000
Location becomes a property of the provenance of the claim, basically a risk metric for the

51:45.000 --> 51:46.640
bank, not a property of the money itself.

51:47.500 --> 51:50.960
Okay, so we've stripped away the magical decaying spatial currency.

51:51.240 --> 51:54.580
We've stripped away the literal printing of money by zoning boards.

51:54.980 --> 51:55.100
Yep.

51:55.260 --> 52:00.580
We are left with a surprisingly sober system of amortizing loans denominated in normal fiat

52:00.580 --> 52:06.760
currency, underwritten strictly by the localized replacement cost of certified physical improvements.

52:07.320 --> 52:10.960
Which brings us to the final and perhaps most difficult hurdle, governance.

52:11.300 --> 52:11.980
Ah, governance.

52:12.300 --> 52:17.000
If this system is so pragmatic and relies on real world measurements, who actually controls

52:17.000 --> 52:17.160
it?

52:17.420 --> 52:21.160
Right, because earlier we talked about the oracle problem, the bribeable inspector.

52:21.160 --> 52:26.340
But the governance issue goes much, much deeper than one corrupt guy with a clipboard.

52:26.340 --> 52:30.920
The crypto world, which we contrasted this with earlier, loves to claim that algorithms

52:30.920 --> 52:32.680
completely remove human discretion.

52:33.080 --> 52:34.080
Code is law, they say.

52:34.220 --> 52:34.940
Code is law.

52:35.200 --> 52:35.580
Exactly.

52:35.940 --> 52:38.280
If it's in the smart contract, it's objective.

52:38.840 --> 52:43.020
But this paper takes a very different, much more realistic stance.

52:43.180 --> 52:44.980
A refreshingly honest stance, I'd say.

52:44.980 --> 52:50.200
The paper explicitly states a principle that literally anyone designing systems should memorize.

52:51.120 --> 52:54.840
Rule-based issuance is not discretion-free issuance.

52:55.440 --> 52:57.360
Discretion hasn't been eliminated by math.

52:57.620 --> 52:59.400
It is simply migrated upstream.

53:00.360 --> 53:04.880
So, if the algorithm executing the loan isn't biased, who is?

53:05.060 --> 53:07.700
Where does the human bias live in this system?

53:08.180 --> 53:11.400
Look closely at the parameters required to run this system.

53:11.400 --> 53:17.060
Who chooses which edition of the International Building Code applies to a specific town?

53:17.400 --> 53:20.760
Who officially constructs and audits the replacement cost index?

53:21.120 --> 53:24.420
Like, which suppliers do they survey and which do they ignore?

53:24.960 --> 53:25.080
Right.

53:25.280 --> 53:28.940
Who accredits the inspectors and decides what constitutes a passing grade?

53:29.480 --> 53:32.860
Who sits on the appellate board that decides the financial consequences

53:32.860 --> 53:35.820
if a fraudulent attestation is discovered years later?

53:36.260 --> 53:39.980
Who sets the haircut ratio on the collateral for the banks?

53:39.980 --> 53:46.940
Every single one of those is a deeply human decision with massive economy-altering monetary consequences.

53:47.700 --> 53:52.620
If the concrete industry lobby gets a seat on the board that defines the replacement cost index,

53:53.000 --> 53:57.880
they can literally tweak the formula to artificially inflate the credit available for pouring concrete.

53:58.120 --> 53:58.900
They absolutely could.

53:59.480 --> 54:04.200
Or if a conservative city council chooses to stick with an outdated 20-year-old building code,

54:04.560 --> 54:09.540
they completely manipulate the eligibility of old structures versus new green technology.

54:09.540 --> 54:10.180
Exactly.

54:11.240 --> 54:18.760
Calling these decisions technical or engineering standards does not remove the incredible economic authority they exercise.

54:19.220 --> 54:24.820
It just places a massive monetary authority inside a boring bureaucratic technical office.

54:24.940 --> 54:25.720
Which makes it invisible.

54:26.020 --> 54:26.260
Right.

54:26.540 --> 54:31.060
The political economy questions who has power, who benefits, who decides,

54:31.420 --> 54:33.220
aren't a supplement to the system design.

54:33.220 --> 54:34.800
They are the design.

54:35.140 --> 54:39.000
The author is very clear that you cannot build a purely objective financial system

54:39.000 --> 54:43.160
because the parameters of measurement are always chosen by subjective humans.

54:43.620 --> 54:48.780
So hearing all of this, the complex replacement costs, the risk topologies, the upstream political fights,

54:49.260 --> 54:52.240
how hard is it to actually build this thing?

54:52.400 --> 54:59.660
Does a country need to completely overthrow the Federal Reserve and redesign its entire national banking system from scratch just to try it?

54:59.660 --> 55:00.280
No.

55:00.940 --> 55:05.680
And that is perhaps the most pragmatic, optimistic takeaway of the entire essay.

55:06.180 --> 55:13.520
The author outlines a five-level nested hierarchy of institutional commitments ranging from L0 to L4.

55:13.520 --> 55:22.000
And they actually prove logically that you don't need to reach the utopian complex levels to start reaping the core benefits of the system.

55:22.840 --> 55:25.800
I think we need a concrete example to really understand this hierarchy.

55:26.340 --> 55:28.440
Let's invent a hypothetical mid-sized city.

55:28.820 --> 55:30.380
Let's call it Springfield.

55:30.860 --> 55:31.100
Okay.

55:31.460 --> 55:31.860
Springfield.

55:32.300 --> 55:37.060
Springfield wants to fix its housing affordability crisis and stop speculative land bubbles.

55:37.660 --> 55:40.900
Walk us through how Springfield would implement these levels step by step.

55:40.900 --> 55:41.460
Okay.

55:41.660 --> 55:44.100
Let's start with Springfield at L0.

55:44.720 --> 55:46.620
L0 is the absolute baseline.

55:47.260 --> 55:49.100
It is simply an attestation registry.

55:49.780 --> 55:50.480
So just a list.

55:50.940 --> 55:51.240
Basically.

55:52.080 --> 55:54.980
Springfield's mayor doesn't touch the banks or the currency at all.

55:55.520 --> 56:07.540
They just instruct the city's building department to create a geographically located, append-only, cryptographically secured digital ledger of every single certified physical transformation that happens in the city.

56:08.120 --> 56:08.720
Every permit.

56:09.140 --> 56:09.700
Every permit.

56:09.700 --> 56:11.060
Every inspection.

56:11.520 --> 56:15.620
Every square foot of new capacity is logged immutably with its physical coordinates.

56:16.460 --> 56:21.080
A city could build this L0 registry tomorrow using standard off-the-shelf database software.

56:21.600 --> 56:24.400
It's just a highly secure public record of physical truth.

56:25.080 --> 56:25.220
Okay.

56:25.360 --> 56:28.320
So Springfield has a perfect map of what exists.

56:28.740 --> 56:29.060
Now what?

56:29.600 --> 56:33.340
Now they move to L1, which introduces collateral eligibility.

56:33.340 --> 56:42.220
Springfield's local credit unions agree to use that L0 ledger to define which of their loans qualify as secured by admissible physical collateral.

56:42.720 --> 56:50.600
They agree to base their internal risk metrics on the physical reality recorded in the registry, applying standardized haircuts to that physical value.

56:50.600 --> 56:54.720
So then L2 is where the rubber actually meets the road financially, I assume?

56:54.720 --> 56:55.280
Yes.

56:55.920 --> 56:59.560
L2 introduces the actual transformation-linked credit issuance.

57:00.280 --> 57:10.080
This is where Springfield's local banks start explicitly issuing amortizing loans to builders and homeowners based only on the replacement cost increments calculated from the L0 registry.

57:10.080 --> 57:17.960
So they completely stop relying on subjective Zillow appraisals and start issuing loans based on the verified costs of the new drywall and plumbing.

57:18.280 --> 57:18.760
Exactly.

57:19.140 --> 57:20.560
And what about L3?

57:21.040 --> 57:23.300
L3 adds the graph-structured clearing.

57:23.900 --> 57:26.540
That's the systemic risk topology we discussed earlier.

57:27.240 --> 57:37.460
Springfield's banking regulators use the geographic data in the ledger to ensure that no local bank is overly exposed to a single neighborhood's power grid or a specific floodplain.

57:37.460 --> 57:40.820
It's basically a localized systemic risk management tool.

57:41.620 --> 57:42.700
And finally, L4.

57:43.400 --> 57:53.940
L4 is the spatially differentiated units, that exotic, decaying spatial currency that we and the author concluded was probably a terrible idea due to Gresham's law.

57:54.320 --> 58:00.080
So the real beauty of this nested property is that the failure or rejection of L4 doesn't break L1 or L2.

58:00.780 --> 58:03.600
Springfield doesn't need to invent a new currency that decays.

58:03.600 --> 58:07.260
They don't need to change national monetary policy at the Federal Reserve.

58:07.460 --> 58:07.920
Not at all.

58:08.440 --> 58:22.080
They just need to implement L1 and L2, maintaining a rigorous registry of physical transformations, computing standardized replacement costs, and encouraging local banks to issue normal USD loans against those specific physical increments.

58:22.080 --> 58:34.020
And just by doing that, Springfield would insulate a massive portion of their local housing finance market from the speculative land price feedback loops that are destroying affordability globally.

58:34.280 --> 58:35.400
That's amazing.

58:35.400 --> 58:49.200
They would replace a market value-dependent recognition of collateral, which is inherently speculative and volatile, with a provenance-dependent recognition of collateral, which is grounded completely in physical reality.

58:49.200 --> 58:55.240
It's a completely different, much healthier paradigm for what debt actually represents in a community.

58:55.960 --> 59:01.600
Okay, let's pull all of this together because we have been on quite an intellectual journey over the last hour.

59:01.600 --> 59:10.640
We started with what sounded like a wild, almost childishly utopian pitch to turn buildings into literal circulating money.

59:10.640 --> 59:16.020
And we watched the author rigorously, ruthlessly, rip their own idea apart.

59:16.740 --> 59:28.080
They exposed the missing numeraire that would cause deflation, the horrific zoning loopholes that would corrupt city councils, and the absolute inevitability of Gresham's law destroying a spatial currency.

59:28.080 --> 59:44.780
But out of that intellectual destruction, they rebuilt something highly practical, a robust system for transformation-backed credit, a system based on physical, auditable truth, rather than the speculative, screeching feedback loop of market appraisals.

59:44.780 --> 01:00:00.340
It is a blueprint for an economy that structurally, mathematically rewards those who actually build, maintain, and improve the real world, rather than just rewarding those who simply sit passively on a piece of land while the community makes it appreciate in value.

01:00:00.820 --> 01:00:03.760
And that is why this dense academic paper matters so much.

01:00:04.040 --> 01:00:06.960
It's not just a theoretical exercise in ledger design.

01:00:07.160 --> 01:00:09.960
It forces us to ask a really fundamental question.

01:00:10.900 --> 01:00:14.120
What are our financial ledgers actually reflecting?

01:00:14.120 --> 01:00:14.780
Right.

01:00:15.100 --> 01:00:16.540
Are they reflecting reality?

01:00:17.060 --> 01:00:21.240
The sweat, the labor, the concrete, the actual human effort of building a civilization?

01:00:22.100 --> 01:00:27.220
Or are they just reflecting our collective, highly leveraged hallucinations about price?

01:00:27.700 --> 01:00:32.260
It demands that the ledger points back to somewhere, to something real.

01:00:32.960 --> 01:00:38.860
It demands that finance serves the physical world rather than the physical world being a playground for finance.

01:00:38.860 --> 01:00:44.280
Which leaves me with a final, slightly provocative thought for you to mull over as we wrap up today.

01:00:44.600 --> 01:00:57.060
If we can build an entire, robust credit architecture around certified physical transformations of the built environment simply to escape speculative finance, what else could we apply this logic to?

01:00:57.060 --> 01:00:59.200
That is the exact right question to ask.

01:00:59.620 --> 01:01:03.600
Once you have the tool of provenance-backed credit, where else can you point it?

01:01:04.040 --> 01:01:04.600
Think about it.

01:01:04.840 --> 01:01:11.440
Could we have a massive credit system issued against certified, scientifically verified ecological restoration?

01:01:11.440 --> 01:01:18.580
I don't mean carbon credits traded on some speculative Wall Street exchange where the price constantly fluctuates based on trader sentiment.

01:01:18.640 --> 01:01:20.040
No, real physical restoration.

01:01:20.160 --> 01:01:20.440
Right.

01:01:20.680 --> 01:01:36.140
I mean amortizing loans issued directly against the physical, verified reality of a restored wetland, or a replanted forest, or a remediated toxic site, based on the physical replacement cost of the labor and biology required to achieve it.

01:01:36.220 --> 01:01:36.620
Absolutely.

01:01:36.620 --> 01:01:43.120
What about human capital? Could we issue credit against certified, verified skill acquisitions?

01:01:43.460 --> 01:01:47.100
The theoretical framework absolutely holds up for those applications.

01:01:47.780 --> 01:02:03.840
The core innovation of this paper, replacing subjective market appraisal with attested physical provenance, is entirely agnostic to the type of transformation, just as long as the transformation can be objectively measured, verified by an oracle, and tracked on an append-only ledger.

01:02:03.840 --> 01:02:10.400
The real revolution of this paper isn't just about real estate, or housing affordability, or stopping the mortgage doom loop.

01:02:10.640 --> 01:02:19.180
It's about forcing our financial systems, our ledgers, to finally actually tell the objective truth about the physical world we all share.

01:02:19.800 --> 01:02:32.640
So the next time you walk past a noisy construction site, or look down at a newly reinforced concrete foundation, or even just stare at the digital numbers in your banking app, ask yourself, what is actually backing it?

01:02:32.640 --> 01:02:33.440
Is it real?

01:02:33.900 --> 01:02:42.880
Right. Is it a political promise? Is it a leveraged speculation? Or could it be a certified, undeniable physical reality?

01:02:43.420 --> 01:02:47.620
Thank you for taking this deep dive with us today. Keep questioning the systems around you.

